<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0-windows;net10.0-windows</TargetFrameworks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Encoding.CodePages" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="StyleCop.Analyzers" Version="1.2.0-beta.556">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <CodeAnalysisRuleSet>..\Rules.ruleset</CodeAnalysisRuleSet>
    <PackageId>RestrictedProcess.NET</PackageId>
    <Version>2.0.0</Version>
    <Authors>Nikolay.IT</Authors>
    <Company />
    <Product>RestrictedProcess.NET</Product>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/NikolayIT/RestrictedProcess.NET</PackageProjectUrl>
    <Description>A .NET library containing restricted process executor which can be used to run Windows programs with restricted rights. RestrictedProcess.NET can also limit the time and the memory of a running process.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>Version 2.0 rebuilds the sandbox on the current Windows security primitives. BREAKING: targets net8.0-windows and net10.0-windows (netstandard2.0 is dropped, so .NET Framework hosts are no longer supported); IExecutor is async-first and takes an ExecutionRequest carrying separate processor-time and wall-clock limits; limits are 64-bit. Security: every group in the token becomes deny-only except a per-level exception list, each run gets a unique SID used both as a restricting SID and as the only non-user entry in a locked-down default DACL, and the new WriteRestricted, StrictlyRestricted and Lockdown token levels join Limited and Restricted. The throwaway desktop is built with a least-privilege DACL and a mandatory label derived from the configured integrity level. The job object is attached at process creation, 128-bit process mitigation policies are supported, shell-brokered breakaway is blocked, and optional AppContainer/LPAC network blocking no longer leaves a permanent ACL grant on the executable. Measurement: job accounting and I/O completion port notifications replace polling, processor time covers the whole process tree including children that already exited, and memory defaults to peak committed bytes. Fixes: CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB is gone (it made process creation fail inside any job that forbids breakaway, including CI runners); command lines are escaped to CommandLineToArgvW rules; exit code 259 is no longer mistaken for a running process; output is drained to end of file rather than abandoned after 100 ms; and a run stopped at its time limit is no longer reported as a runtime error just because the program wrote to standard error. Diagnostics: SandboxException names the step that failed, and SandboxCapabilities.Probe() reports what the host supports.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>true</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <Copyright>Nikolay.IT</Copyright>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/NikolayIT/RestrictedProcess.NET</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>Restricted, Process, Sandbox, Windows Executable</PackageTags>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleToAttribute">
      <_Parameter1>RestrictedProcess.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="..\stylecop.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
